  very anaerobic so low oxygen conditions  and one of the archaea that's highly  prevalent in these soils  is methanopyridines which is an archaea  that can actually  consume methane and so in comparison to  the dna sequence of  methanol paradigms these newly  identified sequences  have a low gc content and so that  suggests that this is something novel  and not just some additional dna  that's part of methanol paradigms and in  addition  they also identified a lot of protein  coding genes in these extra chromosomal  elements  with a lot of them being metabolism  relevant genes  interestingly a majority of the genes  were also novel  and we'll come on to that maybe a little  bit later but actually around 21  of the predicted proteins from these  genes had close matches to proteins of  archaea  most notably to methanol paradigms  however when they looked at the  relationship between  these genetic elements and coexisting  methanol paradigms  they didn't really see a consistent  relationship  and in fact in some cases the abundance  of these novel genetic elements  were up to eight times more abundance  than methanol paradigms  but it raised the idea that these  protein sequences  must have somehow been acquired from  these alkal bilateral gene transfer  and so with this information and the  fact that the characteristics of these  genetic elements  don't have much overlap with other extra  chromosomal elements such as ferrous  plasmids and mini chromosomes  they concluded that these genomes must  represent novel  archaeal extra chromosomal elements that  occur in association  with but not as part of  methanoparadine's genomes  and now for the notable sentence we  refer to these as borgs  a name that reflects their propensity to  assimilate genes from organisms  most notably methanopyridines  so to elaborate a little the data was  suggestive  of these extra chromosomal elements or  borgs  having the ability to inhabit  my family paradigms and to accumulate  some of the